      "id": "GENUS_ANTHOSCOPUS",
      "name": "Anthoscopus",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "African penduline tits — tiny, mouse-grey birds of savanna and woodland. They construct the most elaborate nests of any bird: a felted pouch with a false entrance that the bird must push open from below. The entrance is so well-camouflaged that predators cannot find it. Six species across sub-Saharan Africa.",
      "lineage": "African Penduline Tits",
